'''Yao Ming''' ({{lang-zh|c=姚明}}; born September 12, 1980) is a Chinese [[basketball]] executive and former professional player. He played for the [[Shanghai Sharks]] of the [[Chinese Basketball Association]] (CBA), and then spent his entire nine-year [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA) career with the [[Houston Rockets]]. Yao was selected to start for the [[Western Conference (NBA)|Western Conference]] in the [[NBA All-Star Game]] eight times, and was named to the [[All-NBA Team]] five times. During his final season, he was the [[List of tallest players in National Basketball Association history|tallest active player in the NBA]], at {{convert|7|ft|6|in}}.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nba.com/playerfile/yao_ming/index.html |title=NBA.com Yao Ming Info Page |publisher=NBA |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130629174229/http://www.nba.com/playerfile/yao_ming/index.html |archive-date=June 29, 2013}}</ref>

Yao, who was born in [[Shanghai]], started playing for the Sharks as a teenager, and played on their senior team for five years in the CBA, winning a championship in his final year. After negotiating with the CBA and the Sharks to secure his release, Yao was selected by the Rockets as the first overall pick in the [[2002 NBA draft]]. He reached the [[NBA playoffs]] four times, and the Rockets won the first-round series in the [[2009 NBA playoffs|2009 postseason]], their first playoff series victory since 1997. In July 2011, Yao announced his retirement from professional basketball because of a series of foot and ankle injuries which forced him to miss 250 games in his last six seasons.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Passa |first=Dennis |url=https://news.yahoo.com/chinese-great-yao-ming-retires-basketball-060633532.html |title=Chinese great Yao Ming retires from basketball |agency=Associated Press |publisher=Yahoo! Sports |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111130114954/http://news.yahoo.com/chinese-great-yao-ming-retires-basketball-060633532.html |archive-date=November 30, 2011 |url-status=dead}}</ref> In eight seasons with the Rockets, Yao ranks sixth among franchise leaders in total points and total rebounds, and second in total blocks.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chinese star Houston Rockets' Yao Ming retires, ending basketball career |url=https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/6785935 |publisher=ESPN |date=July 14, 2013 |access-date=July 11, 2020}}</ref>

Yao is one of China's best-known athletes internationally, with sponsorships with several major companies. His rookie year in the NBA was the subject of a documentary film, ''[[The Year of the Yao]]'', and he co-wrote, along with NBA analyst [[Ric Bucher]], an autobiography titled ''Yao: A Life in Two Worlds''. Known in China as the "Yao Ming Phenomenon" and in the United States as the "Ming Dynasty", Yao's success in the NBA, and his popularity among fans, made him a symbol of a new China that was both more modern and more confident.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Pomfret |first1=John |title=The Beautiful Country and the Middle Kingdom: American and China, 1776 to the Present |page=582 |location=New York |publisher=Picador |year=2016 |isbn=978-1-250-16063-8}}</ref> Yao is also an entrepreneur and owner of Yao Family Wines in [[Napa Valley, California]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rothbaum |first=Noah |date=2021-05-26 |title=From the NBA to Napa: Talking Cabernet Wines with Yao Ming |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/10-rounds-with-retired-nba-star-yao-ming-and-owner-of-yao-family-wines |access-date=2024-05-09 |work=The Daily Beast |language=en}}</ref>

In April 2016, Yao was elected into the [[Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame]], alongside [[Shaquille O'Neal]] and [[Allen Iverson]], becoming the first Chinese national to be inducted into the Hall of Fame.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.hoophall.com/news/2016/4/4/naismith-memorial-basketball-hall-of-fame-class-of-2016-anno.html |title=Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame Class of 2016 Announcement presented by Haggar Clothing Company |work=Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame |date=April 4, 2016 |access-date=April 4, 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160417204345/http://www.hoophall.com/news/2016/4/4/naismith-memorial-basketball-hall-of-fame-class-of-2016-anno.html |archive-date=April 17, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-03 |title=Basketball Legend Yao Ming to Speak at NYU Shanghai 8th Undergraduate Commencement |url=https://shanghai.nyu.edu/news/basketball-legend-yao-ming-speak-nyu-shanghai-8th-undergraduate-commencement |access-date=2024-05-17 |website=shanghai.nyu.edu |language=en-US}}</ref> In February 2017, Yao was unanimously elected as chairman of the [[Chinese Basketball Association (organisation)|Chinese Basketball Association]].<ref name="cbachairman">{{cite web |url=https://www.nba.com/article/2017/02/23/yao-ming-named-president-chinese-basketball-association |title=Former Houston Rockets star Yao Ming named president of Chinese Basketball Association |access-date=December 19, 2018 |date=February 23, 2017 |website=NBA.com}}</ref> Yao had a storied career as a member of the Chinese national team.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wells |first=Adam |title=Video: Rockets Legend Yao Ming Inducted into FIBA Hall of Fame Class of 2023 |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/10087125-video-rockets-legend-yao-ming-inducted-into-fiba-hall-of-fame-class-of-2023 |access-date=2024-05-06 |website=Bleacher Report |language=en}}</ref> With the national team, Yao won the FIBA Asia Cup in 2001, 2003, and 2005, winning MVP of the tournament all three times.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2013-09-02 |title=Asian Legends Series: Yao Ming |url=https://www.fiba.basketball/news/asian-legends-series-yao-ming |access-date=2024-05-06 |website=FIBA.basketball |language=en}}</ref> He also made the All-Tournament Team at the FIBA World Cup in 2002. Yao retired from the Chinese national team after the [[2008 Beijing Olympics]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=BLINEBURY |first=FRAN |title=Yao, China come to quiet end with loss to Lithuania |url=https://www.chron.com/sports/rockets/article/yao-china-come-to-quiet-end-with-loss-to-1787233.php |access-date=2024-05-06 |work=Chron |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite Sports-Reference}}</ref>

