'''Wesley Lavon Person''' (born March 28, 1971) is an American former professional [[basketball]] player who played in the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). After his playing career, Person became a women's basketball  assistant coach and then the head men's basketball coach at [[Enterprise-Ozark Community College]].<ref name=chuckperson>{{Cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/nba/columns/story?id=5278158|title=McMenamin: Person more than just a mentor|date=June 12, 2010|website=ESPN.com|access-date=December 6, 2021}}</ref> He was fired from the latter position in 2010.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.al.com/sports/2010/07/enterprise_state_fires_former.html|title=Enterprise State fires former Auburn star Wesley Person as basketball coach|date=July 29, 2010|website=Al.com|access-date=December 6, 2021}}</ref>

Selected by the [[Phoenix Suns]] 23rd overall in the [[1994 NBA draft]] out of [[Auburn University]], Person played for seven teams throughout his career. He played three seasons for the Suns, five for the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]], 82 games over two seasons for the [[Memphis Grizzlies]], 33 games for the [[Portland Trail Blazers]], nine games for the [[Atlanta Hawks]], sixteen games for the [[Miami Heat]] and 25 for the [[Denver Nuggets]]. Person retired with career averages of 11.2 points, 3.3 rebounds, and 1.7 assists per game. According to Complex Sports, Person is the 13th greatest three-point shooter of all time. <ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/sports/2014/03/greatest-three-point-shooters-nba-history/|title=The 25 Greatest Three-Point Shooters in NBA History|website=Complex.com|access-date=December 6, 2021}}</ref>

